# Test: `ddos local max-mitigation-duration` bounds an on-host drop rule that no
# AttackCleared will ever lift, proven by an nft readback and by `show ddos
# local` while the flood is still running.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-ddos-timing-leaves-reach-no-worker AC-6 -- with
#            `max-mitigation-duration 5`, the drop rule ddos-local installs for a
#            box-owned victim is removed by the plugin's worker
#            (register.go startMaxDurationWorker -> responder.go
#            enforceMaxDuration -> removeMitigation), the kernel no longer holds
#            the rule, and `show ddos local` reports no mitigation.
# PREVENTS:  the leaf going back to being parsed, range-checked and read by
#            nothing. removeMitigation has two other callers, the suppress branch
#            of applyMitigation and onCleared, and neither is a timer: before
#            this worker an attack that never cleared held an nftables drop for
#            the life of the daemon while the operator had set a cap that said it
#            would not.
#
# WHY THE FLOOD NEVER STOPS. The removal is asserted while the probe is still
# flooding the victim, so no AttackCleared can have arrived: the detector needs
# clear-consecutive-checks evaluations under the threshold, which the config puts
# at 100. The cap is therefore the only path that could have removed the rule,
# which is what makes the assertion discriminate. The detector emits
# AttackDetected once per generation at onset, so nothing re-installs the rule
# after the cap lifts it.
#
# WHY THE CAP IS 5 AND NOT 1. The probe must SEE the rule installed before it can
# see it removed, and the rule lives for the cap plus at most one worker tick. At
# 1 second that window is one to two seconds; at 5 it is five to six, which the
# probe's 250ms poll cannot miss. The value proves the same code either way: the
# cap is read from the leaf and compared against the wall clock.
#
# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU integration test; NOT executable on the darwin dev host.
#   It needs a fully privileged host and two capability families.
#     - net-admin: ddos-local writes the nftables table, and the probe reads it
#       back through netlink.
#     - bpf (CAP_BPF + CAP_SYS_RESOURCE): the traffic-usage block drives
#       rlimit.RemoveMemlock() and link.AttachTCX()
#       (internal/plugins/trafficusage/attach_linux.go). Without it the victim
#       prefix never resolves, and applyMitigation refuses to install an
#       unscoped rule -- so the test could only fail.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf
# Serialize against every other ddos test: they all flood the SAME loopback
# interface and the detector picks the victim by top-destination-bytes over that
# interface, so a sibling's concurrent flood is indistinguishable from this
# test's own. Rationale in full: test/plugin/ddos-direction.ci.
option=exclusive:group=ddos-flood
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.detect:value=info
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.local:value=info
# 50s to install the drop under a flood, then 40s for the one-second cap worker
# to reach it, plus daemon startup.
option=timeout:value=140s

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
ddos {
	detect {
		enabled true
		absolute-floor 1000
		confirm-duration 1
		clear-consecutive-checks 100
		startup-grace 0
		baseline-window 10
		check-interval 1
		characterize-enable false
	}
	observe {
		incident-ring-size 100
	}
	local {
		response-level enforce
		max-mitigation-duration 5
	}
}

traffic {
	usage {
		enabled true
		track-ip true
		interfaces {
			interface lo {
				enabled true
			}
		}
	}
}

plugin {
	external ddos-local-max-duration-probe {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/ddos-local-max-duration-driver"
		encoder json
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=135s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=LOCAL-CAP-INSTALLED 127.0.0.9
expect=stderr:contains=LOCAL-CAP-REMOVED 127.0.0.9
expect=stderr:contains=ddos-local: drop rule installed
expect=stderr:contains=ddos-local: max-mitigation-duration reached
